;cell division protein FtsK;WD40 repeats;DNA segregation ATPase FtsK SpoIIIE and related;COG1674 DNA segregation ATPase FtsK SpoIIIE and related proteins;four-way junction helicase activity;Forkhead associated domain;Psort location CytoplasmicMembrane, score;Cell division protein ftsk;FtsK SpoIIIE family;FtsK SpoIIIE;chaperone-mediated protein folding;ATP binding P-loop motif. it is found in the FtsK cell division protein from E. coli and the stage III sporulation protein E SpoIIIE, which has roles in regulation of prespore specific gene expression in B. subtilis. a mutation in FtsK causes a temperature sensitive block in cell division and it is involved in peptidoglycan synthesis or modification. the SpoIIIE protein is implicated in intercellular chromosomal DNA transfer;Belongs to the FtsK SpoIIIE SftA family;SMART ATPase, AAA type, core;Domain of unknown function DUF87;FtsK SpoIIIE family protein;Ftsk_gamma;AAA ATPase domain;Protein involved in ATP binding, DNA binding, nucleotide binding, cell cycle, cell division and chromosome segregation;DNA segregation ATPase FtsK SpoIIIE and related proteins;Virulence-associated protein E;PFAM FtsK SpoIIIE family;Caspase domain;DNA segregation ATPase FtsK SpoIIIE;May be involved in recombination;DNA translocase ftsK;COG1674 DNA segregation ATPase FtsK SpoIIIE and related;DNA segregation ATPase, FtsK SpoIIIE family;4TM region of DNA translocase FtsK/SpoIIIE;Inner membrane component of T3SS, cytoplasmic domain;TraM recognition site of TraD and TraG;Essential cell division protein that coordinates cell division and chromosome segregation. The N-terminus is involved in assembly of the cell-division machinery. The C-terminus functions as a DNA motor that moves dsDNA in an ATP-dependent manner towards the dif recombination site, which is located within the replication terminus region. Translocation stops specifically at Xer-dif sites, where FtsK interacts with the Xer recombinase, allowing activation of chromosome unlinking by recombination. FtsK orienting polar sequences (KOPS) guide the direction of DNA translocation. FtsK can remove proteins from DNA as it translocates, but translocation stops specifically at XerCD-dif site, thereby preventing removal of XerC and XerD from dif (By similarity);FtsK/SpoIIIE family;cell division protein
